<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Nakhichevan Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1828 CE · capital Nakhichevan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A khanate on the middle Araxes ruled by the Kangarlu tribe, ceded by Persia to Russia in 1828 and the forerunner of today's Nakhchivan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah's</a> death in 1747, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haydar-quli-khan">Haydar Quli Khan</a> of the Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangarlu">Kangarlu</a> tribe declared himself khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a>, an old trading town on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. The khanate covered the valleys along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> between Erivan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans relied on their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangarlu">Kangarlu</a> tribesmen and on Armenian and Muslim farmers growing grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and fruit with irrigation, while the town traded salt from nearby mines. Their small state was squeezed between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/erivan-khanate">Erivan Khanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karabakh-khanate">Karabakh Khanate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Muhammad Khan Qajar</a> had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalbali-khan">Kalbali Khan</a> blinded for his resistance.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1826-1828">Russo-Persian War</a> of 1826-1828, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehsan-khan-kangarlu">Ehsan Khan Kangarlu</a> went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> in 1828 ceded the khanate to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, which joined it with Erivan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-oblast">Armenian Oblast</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nakhichevan-khanate.jpg" alt="Nakhichevan Khanate" width="320" height="262"><figcaption>Image: www.armenica.org · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ErevanKhanate.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1828 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nakhichevan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Haydar Quli Khan, Kalbali Khan, Ehsan Khan Kangarlu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani; Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Nakhichevan, Nakhchivan Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1788&amp;focus=nakhichevan-khanate" aria-label="Nakhichevan Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5806 773 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nakhichevan Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6126 1029L6298 1144M6126 1029L6298 1144M6126 1029L6487 457M6126 1029L6364 1269"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6298 1144h0M6487 457h0M6364 1269h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6126 1029h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6126 1029h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1788&amp;focus=nakhichevan-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
